course: dictionary
lesson: 981
title: Who is a boaster?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A boaster is a person who boasts. That means they talk too proudly about what they have or what they can do, and they often make it sound bigger than it really is. Look at the words around the word to find the clue: «Toad, the greatest boaster on the river bank» tells you Toad is always bragging about himself.
explain: Stories often show boasters being caught out. In Kipling's tale, the Butterfly boasts that if he stamps his foot, a great palace will vanish. His wife is tested, and the clever Balkis helps the truth come out, so the boast is stopped without anyone being shamed. Wise people can stop a boast this way.
explain: When you hear a big claim, check the plain facts. «A MILLION Robux, well, 500» shows the boaster correcting himself, and the corrected number is the one that counts. Ask yourself: what would the plain, true size be?
fact: A boaster is a person who boasts: someone who talks too proudly about what they have or can do.
fact: The plural of boaster is boasters.
fact: Boasters often make things bigger than they really are.
fact: Related words are «boast», «boastful», «show-off», «big-head» (UK informal) and «bragger».
fact: There are two traps: believing every boast, or calling the boaster a liar and starting a fight.
exercise: What is a boaster? | Someone who is always lost | A kind of fish | *Someone who talks too proudly about what they have or can do
exercise: Find and copy the word from «the greatest boaster on the river bank» that means a person who boasts. | *boaster | river | greatest | bank
exercise: Ali says «a MILLION Robux, well, 500». Which number is the real one? | Robux | a MILLION | *500 | Both are equally true
test: Why can a boaster's big talk not be trusted? | Because boasters never talk at all | Because boasters always tell the truth | Because boasters are always quiet | *Because boasters often make things bigger than they really are
test: Which word is a close relative of boaster? | castle | river | bank | *boastful
exam: Which word means much the same as boaster in UK informal speech? | *big-head | glum | dreich | servo
